### What steps can reproduce the bug?

```js
const { execFileSync } = require("child_process");
try {
  execFileSync("nonexistent_binary_xyz_123");
} catch (err) {
  console.log("err.error === err:", err.error === err); // true
  JSON.stringify(err); // throws: cannot serialize cyclic structures
}
```

Also reproducible via `spawnSync` directly:

```js
const { spawnSync } = require("child_process");
const ret = spawnSync("nonexistent_binary_xyz_123");
// Simulate what checkExecSyncError does:
const err = ret.error;
Object.assign(err, ret);
console.log("err.error === err:", err.error === err); // true
```

### What is the expected behavior?

Error objects from failed `execFileSync`/`execSync`/`spawnSync` calls should not contain self-referencing cycles. `JSON.stringify(err)` should work.

### What do you see instead?

`err.error === err` (the error points to itself), so `JSON.stringify(err)` throws `TypeError: JSON.stringify cannot serialize cyclic structures`.

### Additional information

The bug is in `checkExecSyncError` in [`src/js/node/child_process.ts` lines 1016-1018](https://github.com/oven-sh/bun/blob/main/src/js/node/child_process.ts#L1016-L1018):

```typescript
if (ret.error) {
    err = ret.error;          // err IS ret.error
    ObjectAssign(err, ret);   // copies ret.error onto err → err.error = err
}
```

`ret` has shape `{ error: <Error>, status, stdout, stderr, ... }`. After line 1017, `err` and `ret.error` are the same object. `ObjectAssign(err, ret)` copies all of `ret`'s properties onto `err`, including `.error` — so `err.error` points to `err` itself.

Node.js has the [identical code](https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/main/lib/child_process.js) and the same bug.

A minimal fix would be to delete the self-reference after the assign: `delete err.error;` after line 1018.

